ound sound, akin to an \"underground demo tape from a cassette\" rather than a super-production. The album was a musical revolution, particularly in its distinct sampling technique, utilizing deconstructed and broken samples from 40s, 50s, and 60s soul, funk, jazz, and classic soul, setting a new standard for hip hop sampling that influenced later artists like J.C. and Kanye West. It established the group's unique lyrical style, where each of the eight members brought their own distinct attitude, from Ghostface Killah's \"swaggering attitude\" to Inspectah Deck's \"reflective and quiet parts,\" using metaphors inspired by martial arts, Shaolin, chess, and warfare. The group had to resort to hustling drugs to finance the recording of this first album, as they received no money from RCA despite signing a landmark contract. \"El disco se hizo, siempre he supuesto sin medios, sin recursos, sin ayuda y de hecho es lo que dio ese sonido tan característico de su mejor butanclan que trae todo ese sonido del Andernal New York inodil Hip Hop y lo trae a un disco de verdad.\"","year":1993,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"Wu Tang Clan. Historia viva del Hip Hop","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-03mEzKqrvA"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The band's initial album release was an absolute success, following RZA's unique contract negotiation that secured solo deals for individual members. Its success initiated RZA's five-year plan for staggered solo album releases. \"Sale el primer disco del Gutanclan es un éxito absoluto y entonces empieza el plan de los 5 años de Reiza.\"","year":1993,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"Wu-Tang Clan como Negocio Musical","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qCEL6NXkIv4"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Cassette Edition","body":"The first album of the Wu-Tang Clan, \"Enter the Wu-Tang (36 Chambers),\" is being released on cassette. This is considered a classic and a recurrent release for Record Store Day. \"este año sale el primer disco de los The Boots and Clan, en el de The Boots and Clan, Certis and Xambas en Caset.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"Record Store Day 2018. Resumen de lanzamientos.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9xo1BWPOGk0"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":254615580851448,"artist":"Wu-Tang Clan","artist_id":137452855677326,"title":"Enter the Wu-Tang (36 Chambers)","year":1993,"ess":1,"yt":"k7eujJUnXR8","kind":null,"book":{"id":2,"title":"The Come Up: An Oral History of the Rise of Hip-Hop","author":"Jonathan Abrams","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/ComeUp/"},"scene":"Wu-Tang Clan","scene_id":137452855677326,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/ComeUp/#wu-tang","chapters":["Cap. 17: Raising the Bar"],"pages":[[1,271,343],[1,453,492]]},{"id":254615580851448,"artist":"Wu-Tang Clan","artist_id":137452855677326,"title":"Enter the Wu-Tang (36 Chambers)","year":1993,"ess":1,"yt":"k7eujJUnXR8","kind":null,"book":{"id":10,"title":"Loops 1 y 2. Una historia de la música electrónica","author":"Javier Blánquez (ed.)","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/loops_timeline/"},"scene":"Boom bap","scene_id":126011890020151,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/loops_timeline/#boom-bap","chapters":["Cap. 14: Equipo en expansión: la nueva escuela del hip hop (1989-2002)"],"pages":[[1,262],[1,263],[1,451]]}],"others":[{"id":89113431206361,"type":"album","name":"Wu-Tang Forever","year":1997,"weight":22},{"id":245218785910459,"type":"album","name":"Once Upon a Time in Shaolin","year":2017,"weight":7},{"id":87125749932619,"type":"song","name":"Impossible","year":1997,"weight":7},{"id":211099777598578,"type":"work","name":"Protect Ya Neck","year":1992,"weight":6},{"id":143433472093446,"type":"album","name":"Enter the Wu-Tang","year":1993,"weight":6},{"id":193099288750202,"type":"album","name":"The Saga Continues Instrumental","year":null,"weight":4},{"id":166207502672810,"type":"song","name":"C.R